OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ----aalliiggnneedd
              Use with --separator to align the output columns

       --aa|----aallll
              Show information about internal LVs.  These are components of
              normal LVs, such as mirrors, which are not independently acces-
              sible, e.g. not mountable.

       ----bbiinnaarryy
              Use binary values "0" or "1" instead of descriptive literal val-
              ues for columns that have exactly two valid values to report
              (not counting the "unknown" value which denotes that the value
              could not be determined).

       --cc|----ccoolloonn
              Generate colon separated output for easier parsing in scripts or
              programs.  Also see vvggss(8) which provides considerably more con-
              trol over the output.

       --CC|----ccoolluummnnss
              Display output in columns, the equivalent of vvggss(8).  Options
              listed are the same as options given in vvggss(8).

       ----ccoommmmaannddpprrooffiillee _S_t_r_i_n_g
              The command profile to use for command configuration.  See
              llvvmm..ccoonnff(5) for more information about profiles.

       ----ccoonnffiigg _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Config settings for the command. These override lvm.conf set-
              tings.  The String arg uses the same format as lvm.conf, or may
              use section/field syntax.  See llvvmm..ccoonnff(5) for more information
              about config.

       ----ccoonnffiiggrreeppoorrtt lloogg|vvgg|llvv|ppvv|ppvvsseegg|sseegg
              See llvvmmrreeppoorrtt(7).

       --dd|----ddeebbuugg ...
              Set debug level. Repeat from 1 to 6 times to increase the detail
              of messages sent to the log file and/or syslog (if configured).

       ----ddrriivveerrllooaaddeedd yy|nn
              If set to no, the command will not attempt to use device-mapper.
              For testing and debugging.

       ----ffoorreeiiggnn
              Report/display foreign VGs that would otherwise be skipped.  See
              llvvmmssyysstteemmiidd(7) for more information about foreign VGs.

       --hh|----hheellpp
              Display help text.

       --HH|----hhiissttoorryy
              Include historical LVs in the output.  (This has no effect un-
              less LVs were removed while lvm.conf metadata/record_lvs_history
              was enabled.

       ----iiggnnoorreelloocckkiinnggffaaiilluurree
              Allows a command to continue with read-only metadata operations
              after locking failures.

       ----lloocckkoopptt _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Used to pass options for special cases to lvmlockd.  See llvvmm--
              lloocckkdd(8) for more information.

       ----llooggoonnllyy
              Suppress command report and display only log report.

       ----lloonngghheellpp
              Display long help text.

       --mm|----mmaappss
              Display the mapping of logical extents to PVs and physical ex-
              tents.  To map physical extents to logical extents use: pvs
              --segments -o+lv_name,seg_start_pe,segtype

       ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
              Suppress the headings line that is normally the first line of
              output.  Useful if grepping the output.

       ----nnoolloocckkiinngg
              Disable locking.

       ----nnoossuuffffiixx
              Suppress the suffix on output sizes. Use with --units (except h
              and H) if processing the output.

       --oo|----ooppttiioonnss _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Comma-separated, ordered list of fields to display in columns.
              String arg syntax is: [+|-|#]Field1[,Field2 ...]  The prefix ++
              will append the specified fields to the default fields, -- will
              remove the specified fields from the default fields, and ## will
              compact specified fields (removing them when empty for all
              rows.)  Use --oo hheellpp to view the list of all available fields.
              Use separate lists of fields to add, remove or compact by re-
              peating the -o option: -o+field1,field2 -o-field3,field4
              -o#field5.  These lists are evaluated from left to right.  Use
              field name llvv__aallll to view all LV fields, vvgg__aallll all VG fields,
              ppvv__aallll all PV fields, ppvvsseegg__aallll all PV segment fields, sseegg__aallll
              all LV segment fields, and ppvvsseegg__aallll all PV segment columns.
              See the lvm.conf report section for more config options.  See
              llvvmmrreeppoorrtt(7) for more information about reporting.

       ----pprrooffiillee _S_t_r_i_n_g
              An alias for --commandprofile or --metadataprofile, depending on
              the command.

       --qq|----qquuiieett ...
              Suppress output and log messages. Overrides --debug and --ver-
              bose.  Repeat once to also suppress any prompts with answer
              'no'.

       ----rreeaaddoonnllyy
              Run the command in a special read-only mode which will read on-
              disk metadata without needing to take any locks. This can be
              used to peek inside metadata used by a virtual machine image
              while the virtual machine is running. No attempt will be made to
              communicate with the device-mapper kernel driver, so this option
              is unable to report whether or not LVs are actually in use.

       ----rreeppoorrttffoorrmmaatt bbaassiicc|jjssoonn
              Overrides current output format for reports which is defined
              globally by the report/output_format setting in lvm.conf.  bbaassiicc
              is the original format with columns and rows.  If there is more
              than one report per command, each report is prefixed with the
              report name for identification. jjssoonn produces report output in
              JSON format. See llvvmmrreeppoorrtt(7) for more information.

       ----sseeggmmeennttss

       --SS|----sseelleecctt _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Select objects for processing and reporting based on specified
              criteria.  The criteria syntax is described by ----sseelleecctt hheellpp and
              llvvmmrreeppoorrtt(7).  For reporting commands, one row is displayed for
              each object matching the criteria.  See ----ooppttiioonnss hheellpp for se-
              lectable object fields.  Rows can be displayed with an addi-
              tional "selected" field (-o selected) showing 1 if the row
              matches the selection and 0 otherwise.  For non-reporting com-
              mands which process LVM entities, the selection is used to
              choose items to process.

       ----sseeppaarraattoorr _S_t_r_i_n_g
              String to use to separate each column. Useful if grepping the
              output.

       ----sshhaarreedd
              Report/display shared VGs that would otherwise be skipped when
              lvmlockd is not being used on the host.  See llvvmmlloocckkdd(8) for
              more information about shared VGs.

       --OO|----ssoorrtt _S_t_r_i_n_g
              Comma-separated ordered list of columns to sort by. Replaces the
              default selection. Precede any column with -- for a reverse sort
              on that column.

       --tt|----tteesstt
              Run in test mode. Commands will not update metadata.  This is
              implemented by disabling all metadata writing but nevertheless
              returning success to the calling function. This may lead to un-
              usual error messages in multi-stage operations if a tool relies
              on reading back metadata it believes has changed but hasn't.

       ----uunnbbuuffffeerreedd
              Produce output immediately without sorting or aligning the col-
              umns properly.

       ----uunniittss rr|RR|hh|HH|bb|BB|ss|SS|kk|KK|mm|MM|gg|GG|tt|TT|pp|PP|ee|EE
              All sizes are output in these units: human-(r)eadable with '<'
              rounding indicator, (h)uman-readable, (b)ytes, (s)ectors,
              (k)ilobytes, (m)egabytes, (g)igabytes, (t)erabytes, (p)etabytes,
              (e)xabytes.  Capitalise to use multiples of 1000 (S.I.) instead
              of 1024.  Custom units can be specified, e.g. --units 3M.

       --vv|----vveerrbboossee ...
              Set verbose level. Repeat from 1 to 4 times to increase the de-
              tail of messages sent to stdout and stderr.

       ----vveerrssiioonn
              Display version information.

       --yy|----yyeess
              Do not prompt for confirmation interactively but always assume
              the answer yes. Use with extreme caution.  (For automatic no,
              see -qq.)

VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS
       _V_G
              Volume Group name.  See llvvmm(8) for valid names.

       _L_V
              Logical  Volume  name.  See llvvmm(8) for valid names.  An LV posi-
              tional arg generally includes the VG  name  and  LV  name,  e.g.
              VG/LV.

       _T_a_g
              Tag  name.  See llvvmm(8) for information about tag names and using
              tags in place of a VG, LV or PV.

       _S_t_r_i_n_g
              See the option description for information about the string con-
              tent.

       _S_i_z_e[UNIT]
              Size  is  an  input number that accepts an optional unit.  Input
              units are always treated as base two values, regardless of capi-
              talization,  e.g.  'k'  and 'K' both refer to 1024.  The default
              input unit is specified by letter, followed by |UNIT.  UNIT rep-
              resents  other  possible  input units: bbBBssSSkkKKmmMMggGGttTTppPPeeEE.  b|B is
              bytes, s|S is sectors of 512 bytes, k|K is KiB, m|M is MiB,  g|G
              is GiB, t|T is TiB, p|P is PiB, e|E is EiB.  (This should not be
              confused with the output control --units, where capital  letters
              mean multiple of 1000.)

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS
       See  llvvmm(8)  for  information  about environment variables used by lvm.
       For example, LVM_VG_NAME can generally be substituted for a required VG
       parameter.

